    [38;5;153mindex.js       createLogger(config) -> { debug, info, warn, error, setUser }[39m
    [38;5;153mrecord.js      builds the canonical record (level, message, fields, ts, sessionId, userId)[39m
    [38;5;153mredact.js      allowlist filter + free-text scrubber[39m
    [38;5;153msession.js     session-ID generation and storage[39m
    [38;5;153msinks/[39m
      [38;5;153mconsole.js   dev sink, pretty output[39m
      [38;5;153mstdout.js    Node: one JSON object per line to stdout/stderr[39m
      [38;5;153mhttp.js      Browser: batched POST to the vendor ingest endpoint[39m
    [38;5;153mhandlers.js    installs global error handlers for the current runtime[39m
    [38;5;153mbrowser.js     assembles core + http sink + browser handlers[39m
    [38;5;153mnode.js        assembles core + stdout sink + Node handlers[39m

  The core never knows where logs go — a sink is just [38;5;153m{ write(record) }[39m. That's the interface that makes "swap the
  hosted vendor for our own collector later" a one-file change, which is the thing you bought by picking the shared
  core.

  [1mOn the vendor integration:[0m I'd talk to the vendor's ingest API over plain [38;5;153mfetch[39m rather than installing their browser
  SDK. The SDKs assume a bundler and pull in exactly the auto-instrumentation you just declined. The cost is real — you
  lose source-map-aware stack traces and the SDK's built-in release tracking, so minified stacks will be harder to read.
  Worth naming now rather than discovering it during an incident.

  [1mTooling question, since this repo has none configured.[0m Cheapest to set up before the code exists. Which do you want
  from the start?

  - Linting + formatting (ESLint + Prettier)
  - Unit tests ([38;5;153mnode:test[39m, built in — no dependency)
  - End-to-end tests (would mean a browser harness; a real new dependency)

  My take: ESLint + Prettier and [38;5;153mnode:test[39m. The redaction logic in [38;5;153mredact.js[39m is exactly the kind of code that needs
  tests — a silent regression there sends passwords to a third party — and [38;5;153mnode:test[39m costs nothing. I'd skip e2e for
  now.

  Does Section 1 look right — particularly the ESM conversion and the no-SDK call?

